      <h2>Unit-Wise Important Topics And Repeated Questions</h2>
      <div class="pp-unit"><h3><span class="pp-badge">Unit 1 &bull; 18%</span> Basic Chemistry</h3><p><strong>Paper signal:</strong> Hybridization, Heisenberg uncertainty principle, Schrodinger wave equation, de Broglie concept and hard-soft acid-base concept repeat strongly.</p><div class="pp-grid-2"><div><h4>Most Important Topics</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>de Broglie concept and wave mechanical nature of electron.</li><li>Heisenberg uncertainty principle with statement and expression.</li><li>Schrodinger wave equation and meaning of terms.</li><li>Hybridization types: \(sp\), \(sp^2\), \(sp^3\), \(sp^3d\), \(sp^3d^2\).</li><li>Hard and soft acids and bases with applications.</li></ul></div><div><h4>Most Repeated Questions</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Explain de Broglie''s concept and write expression for wavelength.</li><li>State and explain Heisenberg uncertainty principle with expression and significance.</li><li>Write Schrodinger wave equation and explain each term.</li><li>Explain hybridization types with shapes and examples.</li><li>Compare hard acids/bases with soft acids/bases using examples.</li></ul></div></div><p><strong>Answer-writing note:</strong> For hybridization, write central atom, steric number, hybridization, shape, bond angle and example.</p></div>

      <div class="pp-unit"><h3><span class="pp-badge">Unit 2 &bull; 22%</span> Water Technology And Corrosion</h3><p><strong>Paper signal:</strong> Strongest scoring unit: hardness, boiler problems, zeolite/deionization, corrosion theory, pitting corrosion, inhibitors and cathodic protection.</p><div class="pp-grid-2"><div><h4>Most Important Topics</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Hardness of water, types, units and industrial effects.</li><li>Water softening by zeolite and deionization processes.</li><li>Boiler problems: caustic embrittlement, sludge, scale, priming and foaming.</li><li>Chemical and electrochemical corrosion.</li><li>Pitting corrosion and electrochemical theory of corrosion.</li><li>Corrosion prevention by inhibitors, sacrificial protection and cathodic protection.</li></ul></div><div><h4>Most Repeated Questions</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Explain hard water, industrial effects and remediation methods.</li><li>Explain zeolite process with neat diagram, advantages and disadvantages.</li><li>Explain deionization method with suitable diagram.</li><li>Discuss caustic embrittlement in boilers with chemistry, causes and prevention.</li><li>Differentiate scale and sludge; differentiate priming and foaming.</li><li>Explain electrochemical corrosion, pitting corrosion and corrosion prevention.</li></ul></div></div><p><strong>Answer-writing note:</strong> For boiler problems, write cause, chemistry, effect and prevention. For corrosion, show anode reaction, cathode reaction, electron flow and rust formation.</p></div>

      <div class="pp-unit"><h3><span class="pp-badge">Unit 3 &bull; 22%</span> Material Chemistry</h3><p><strong>Paper signal:</strong> Nanomaterials, fullerenes, carbon nanotubes, nanowires, synthesis methods, biodegradable polymers, alloys and steel heat treatment are high-return.</p><div class="pp-grid-2"><div><h4>Most Important Topics</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Fullerenes, fullerols, carbon nanotubes, nanowires and metal nanoparticles.</li><li>Top-down and bottom-up approaches for nanomaterial synthesis.</li><li>Properties and applications of nanomaterials.</li><li>Biodegradable polymers and bio-based feedstock.</li><li>Metals and alloys: properties, applications and classification.</li><li>Heat treatment and applications of steel.</li></ul></div><div><h4>Most Repeated Questions</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Explain top-down and bottom-up synthesis of nanomaterials.</li><li>Write properties and applications of fullerenes and fullerols.</li><li>Discuss carbon nanotubes and nanowires with uses.</li><li>Write a note on metal nanoparticles with applications.</li><li>Explain biodegradable polymer and bio-based feedstock.</li><li>Explain alloys, copper/aluminum/nickel alloys and heat treatment of steel.</li></ul></div></div><p><strong>Answer-writing note:</strong> For nanomaterials, include definition, method, properties and applications. For alloys, use a composition-property-application table.</p></div>

      <div class="pp-unit"><h3><span class="pp-badge">Unit 4 &bull; 22%</span> Fuel And Batteries</h3><p><strong>Paper signal:</strong> Petroleum refining, bomb calorimeter, calorific value, knocking, octane number, coal analysis, biofuels, lithium batteries and fermentation repeat frequently.</p><div class="pp-grid-2"><div><h4>Most Important Topics</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Refining of petroleum by fractional distillation.</li><li>Calorific value by bomb calorimeter.</li><li>Knocking and octane number.</li><li>Proximate and ultimate analysis of coal.</li><li>Biofuels, biodiesel and biomass briquettes.</li><li>Lithium batteries: construction, working and reactions.</li><li>Fermentation for ethanol and acetic acid.</li></ul></div><div><h4>Most Repeated Questions</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Explain fractional distillation of petroleum with labelled diagram.</li><li>Explain bomb calorimeter and determination of calorific value.</li><li>Define calorific value, GCV and NCV.</li><li>Explain knocking and octane number.</li><li>Differentiate proximate and ultimate analysis of coal.</li><li>Explain lithium battery construction, working, reactions and uses.</li><li>Explain fermentation for ethanol and acetic acid with flow diagram.</li></ul></div></div><p><strong>Answer-writing note:</strong> Fuel answers score better with diagrams: fractional distillation column, bomb calorimeter and fermentation flow diagram.</p></div>

      <div class="pp-unit"><h3><span class="pp-badge">Unit 5 &bull; 16%</span> Instrumental Analysis</h3><p><strong>Paper signal:</strong> UV-visible spectroscopy, IR spectroscopy, NMR spectroscopy, gas chromatography and thin layer chromatography are high-return.</p><div class="pp-grid-2"><div><h4>Most Important Topics</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>UV-visible spectroscopy: principle, instrumentation and applications.</li><li>IR spectroscopy: principle and applications.</li><li>NMR spectroscopy: principle, instrumentation and applications.</li><li>Chromatography principle, mobile phase and stationary phase.</li><li>Thin layer chromatography and retention factor.</li><li>Gas chromatography principle and instrumentation.</li></ul></div><div><h4>Most Repeated Questions</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>What is spectroscopy? Explain its importance in structural elucidation.</li><li>State Lambert-Beer law and derive mathematical expression.</li><li>Explain UV-visible spectroscopy with principle, instrumentation and applications.</li><li>Explain IR spectroscopy and NMR spectroscopy.</li><li>Explain chromatography, TLC, retention factor and gas chromatography.</li><li>Analyze a simple organic compound using UV, IR and NMR spectroscopy.</li></ul></div></div><p><strong>Answer-writing note:</strong> For spectroscopy, always write principle, source, sample holder, detector, output and applications.</p></div>
