    <section class="pp-section">
      <h2>Important Principles, Laws And Theories</h2>
      <div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Principle / Law / Theory</th><th>Core Idea To Write</th><th>Exam Use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>de Broglie matter-wave principle</td><td>Moving particles have wave character; wavelength is inversely proportional to momentum.</td><td>Wave mechanics and electron nature.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Heisenberg uncertainty principle</td><td>Exact position and exact momentum of a microscopic particle cannot be known simultaneously.</td><td>Atomic structure and wave mechanics.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hybridization principle</td><td>Atomic orbitals of comparable energy mix to form equivalent hybrid orbitals with definite geometry.</td><td>Molecular shape and bond-angle questions.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pearson HSAB principle</td><td>Hard acids prefer hard bases and soft acids prefer soft bases, giving more stable combinations.</td><td>Stability, reactivity and classification examples.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ion exchange principle</td><td>Hardness-causing ions are exchanged with harmless ions such as sodium or hydrogen/hydroxyl ions.</td><td>Zeolite and deionization processes.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Electrochemical corrosion theory</td><td>Corrosion occurs through anodic oxidation and cathodic reduction on different areas of metal surface.</td><td>Rusting, pitting and corrosion mechanism.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cathodic protection principle</td><td>The protected metal is forced to behave as cathode, so oxidation occurs elsewhere.</td><td>Sacrificial anode and impressed current protection.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Nanomaterial size-effect principle</td><td>At 1 to 100 nm size, high surface area and quantum effects change material properties.</td><td>Nanomaterial properties and applications.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Alloying principle</td><td>Combining metals or metal with non-metal improves strength, hardness, corrosion resistance or special properties.</td><td>Alloys, steel and engineering materials.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fractional distillation principle</td><td>Petroleum components separate because different fractions have different boiling ranges.</td><td>Refining of crude petroleum.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Calorimetry principle</td><td>Heat released by combustion is absorbed by water and calorimeter; temperature rise gives calorific value.</td><td>Bomb calorimeter.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lithium-ion battery principle</td><td>Lithium ions move reversibly between electrodes during charging and discharging.</td><td>Rechargeable cells and energy storage.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Spectroscopy principle</td><td>Matter absorbs or emits electromagnetic radiation at characteristic energy differences.</td><td>UV-visible, IR and NMR spectroscopy.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Chromatography principle</td><td>Components separate due to different distribution between stationary phase and mobile phase.</td><td>TLC, gas and liquid chromatography.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </section>

