      <div class="pp-unit"><h3><span class="pp-badge">Module 1 &bull; 25%</span> Introduction and Fundamentals of Statics</h3><div class="pp-grid-2"><div><h4>Most Important Topics</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Force, system of forces, composition and resolution.</li><li>Parallelogram law, triangle law, polygon law and Lami theorem.</li><li>Resultant and equilibrant of concurrent force systems.</li><li>Free body diagram and equilibrium conditions.</li><li>Moment, couple, Varignon theorem and force-couple system.</li><li>Coplanar non-concurrent force equilibrium.</li></ul></div><div><h4>Most Repeated Questions</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Define rigid body, deformable body and elastic body.</li><li>State and explain parallelogram law of forces.</li><li>State law of polygon of forces with example.</li><li>State and explain Lami theorem with diagram.</li><li>Find resultant of a coplanar force system in magnitude and direction.</li><li>State and explain Varignon theorem.</li><li>Explain moment, couple and characteristics of a couple.</li><li>Solve equilibrium problems for concurrent and non-concurrent forces.</li></ul></div></div><p><strong>Answer-writing note:</strong> Draw the force diagram first. Resolve forces into horizontal and vertical components and show sign convention clearly.</p></div>

      <div class="pp-unit"><h3><span class="pp-badge">Module 2 &bull; 30%</span> Beams, Shear Force, Bending Moment and Beam Stresses</h3><div class="pp-grid-2"><div><h4>Most Important Topics</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Types of beams, supports and loads.</li><li>Support reactions for statically determinate beams.</li><li>Shear force and bending moment diagrams.</li><li>Point of contraflexure and maximum bending moment.</li><li>Flexural stress and simple bending equation.</li><li>Shear stress distribution in rectangular, circular, T and I sections.</li></ul></div><div><h4>Most Repeated Questions</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Explain types of beams, supports and loads with sketches.</li><li>Determine support reactions for a simply supported beam.</li><li>Draw SFD and BMD for beams with point load, uniformly distributed load and couple.</li><li>Define bending moment and point of contraflexure.</li><li>Derive the bending equation for pure bending.</li><li>Determine bending stress in rectangular, circular, pipe, T or I section.</li><li>Draw shear stress distribution diagrams for rectangular, circular, T and I sections.</li></ul></div></div><p><strong>Answer-writing note:</strong> For beam answers, draw the load diagram, reaction diagram, shear force diagram and bending moment diagram in proper order.</p></div>

      <div class="pp-unit"><h3><span class="pp-badge">Module 3 &bull; 25%</span> Centroid, Moment of Inertia and Torsion</h3><div class="pp-grid-2"><div><h4>Most Important Topics</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Centroid, centre of gravity and centre of mass.</li><li>Centroid of composite sections.</li><li>Moment of inertia of rectangular, triangular, circular and composite areas.</li><li>Parallel and perpendicular axes theorems.</li><li>Radius of gyration and section modulus.</li><li>Torsion assumptions and torsion equation for circular shafts.</li></ul></div><div><h4>Most Repeated Questions</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Differentiate centroid and centre of gravity.</li><li>Find centroid of I-section, channel section or composite area.</li><li>Derive moment of inertia of a rectangle about centroidal axis using first principle.</li><li>Derive perpendicular axis theorem with usual notations.</li><li>Find moment of inertia of composite section about centroidal axes.</li><li>State assumptions made in theory of torsion.</li><li>Derive torsion equation with usual notations.</li><li>Find polar moment and torsional shear stress for solid or hollow circular shaft.</li></ul></div></div><p><strong>Answer-writing note:</strong> Use tabular calculation for centroid and moment of inertia problems; it prevents mistakes and looks professional.</p></div>

      <div class="pp-unit"><h3><span class="pp-badge">Module 4 &bull; 20%</span> Simple Stresses and Strains</h3><div class="pp-grid-2"><div><h4>Most Important Topics</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Normal stress, shear stress and complementary shear.</li><li>Linear, lateral, volumetric and thermal strain.</li><li>Hooke''s law and Poisson ratio.</li><li>Elastic constants and relations between them.</li><li>Axial deformation of uniform and stepped bars.</li><li>Composite and compound bars under axial load.</li></ul></div><div><h4>Most Repeated Questions</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Define stress, strain and Poisson ratio.</li><li>Determine stress, strain and elongation of a bar under axial pull.</li><li>Determine modulus of rigidity, bulk modulus and change in volume from axial loading data.</li><li>Derive relation between elastic constants.</li><li>Solve stepped-bar elongation problem.</li><li>Solve composite bar or reinforced concrete column problem.</li><li>Explain thermal stress and strain.</li><li>Define composite bar and compound bar.</li></ul></div></div><p><strong>Answer-writing note:</strong> Write units carefully: \(N/mm^2\), mm, kN and GPa conversions are where most marks are lost.</p></div>

      <div class="pp-unit"><h3><span class="pp-badge">Module 5 &bull; Lab-focused</span> Material Properties and Simple Machines</h3><div class="pp-grid-2"><div><h4>Most Important Topics</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Stress-strain diagram for ductile material.</li><li>Elastic limit, yield point, ultimate strength, working stress and factor of safety.</li><li>Toughness, hardness, ductility and brittleness.</li><li>Velocity ratio, mechanical advantage and efficiency.</li><li>Law of machines and lifting machines.</li><li>Pulley blocks, screw jack and crab mechanism.</li></ul></div><div><h4>Most Repeated Questions</h4><ul class="pp-list"><li>Explain stress-strain diagram for ductile material.</li><li>Define elastic limit and modulus of elasticity.</li><li>Define hardness, toughness, ductility and brittleness.</li><li>Define velocity ratio, mechanical advantage and efficiency.</li><li>Write law of machine and explain its terms.</li><li>Explain why this module is mostly practical/laboratory focused in the latest syllabus.</li></ul></div></div><p><strong>Answer-writing note:</strong> Keep this as low-priority theory, but do not ignore definitions because recent papers still ask stress-strain diagram and material terms.</p></div>
    </section>

