    <section class="pp-section">
      <h2>Most Important Theorems And Principles</h2>
      <div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Theorem / Principle</th><th>Statement</th><th>Where To Use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Principle of transmissibility</td><td>A force may be transmitted along its line of action without changing the external effect on a rigid body.</td><td>Force systems.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Principle of superposition</td><td>The combined effect of several forces equals the vector sum of individual effects.</td><td>Resultant forces.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Parallelogram law of forces</td><td>If two forces acting at a point are represented by adjacent sides of a parallelogram, the diagonal gives their resultant.</td><td>Resultant of two forces.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Varignon theorem</td><td>Moment of resultant about a point equals algebraic sum of moments of component forces about the same point.</td><td>Moments and support reactions.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Parallel axis theorem</td><td>Moment of inertia about any axis equals centroidal moment of inertia plus area times square of distance between axes.</td><td>Composite moment of inertia.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Perpendicular axis theorem</td><td>For a plane lamina, moment of inertia about an axis perpendicular to the plane equals sum of moments about two mutually perpendicular in-plane axes.</td><td>Moment of inertia derivation.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </section>

    <section class="pp-section">
      <h2>Most Important Derivations</h2>
      <div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Derivation</th><th>Must Include</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>Resultant of coplanar concurrent force system by component method.</td><td>Resolve forces into components, calculate \(R_x\), \(R_y\), \(R\) and direction.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>Lami theorem.</td><td>Triangle of forces, sine rule and equilibrium of three forces.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>Support reaction equations for statically determinate beam.</td><td>Free body diagram, equilibrium equations and sign convention.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>Relationship between loading, shear force and bending moment.</td><td>Small beam element, \( rac{dM}{dx}=V\) and \( rac{dV}{dx}=-w\).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>Bending equation or flexure formula.</td><td>Assumptions, strain variation, stress relation and final \( rac{M}{I}= rac{\sigma}{y}= rac{E}{R}\).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>Maximum shear stress in rectangular section.</td><td>Shear formula, first moment of area and final \(	au_{max}= rac{1.5V}{A}\).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td>Moment of inertia of rectangle about centroidal axis using first principle.</td><td>Elemental strip, integration and centroidal axis result.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8</td><td>Parallel and perpendicular axes theorems.</td><td>Definition of moment of inertia and standard proof.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9</td><td>Torsion equation for circular shaft.</td><td>Assumptions, shear strain, Hooke law in shear and final \( rac{T}{J}= rac{	au}{r}= rac{G	heta}{L}\).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10</td><td>Relations between elastic constants.</td><td>Uniaxial stress state, lateral strain, volumetric strain and relation among \(E\), \(G\), \(K\) and Poisson ratio.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </section>

    <section class="pp-section">
      <h2>Most Important Definitions</h2>
      <div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Definition</th><th>Exam-Ready Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Rigid body</td><td>Body in which distance between any two particles remains unchanged under load.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Deformable body</td><td>Body that changes shape or size under applied load.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Resultant</td><td>Single force that has the same effect as the given force system.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Equilibrant</td><td>Force equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the resultant.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Moment</td><td>Turning effect of a force about a point or axis.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Couple</td><td>Two equal and opposite parallel forces separated by a distance producing pure rotation.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shear force</td><td>Algebraic sum of vertical forces on one side of a beam section.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bending moment</td><td>Algebraic sum of moments of forces on one side of a beam section.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Point of contraflexure</td><td>Point on a beam where bending moment changes sign.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Centroid</td><td>Geometrical centre of an area or line.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Centre of gravity</td><td>Point through which resultant weight of a body acts.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Moment of inertia</td><td>Second moment of area about an axis.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Neutral axis</td><td>Axis in beam section where bending stress is zero.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Section modulus</td><td>Ratio of moment of inertia to distance of extreme fibre from neutral axis.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stress</td><td>Internal resistance per unit area.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Strain</td><td>Deformation per unit original dimension.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Poisson ratio</td><td>Ratio of lateral strain to longitudinal strain.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Torsional rigidity</td><td>Product of modulus of rigidity and polar moment of inertia.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </section>

    <section class="pp-section">
      <h2>Last-Minute Preparation Strategy</h2>
      <div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Time Left</th><th>Do This First</th><th>Avoid This</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Seven days</td><td>Finish statics, beams, SFD/BMD, bending stress, centroid, moment of inertia and stress-strain numericals.</td><td>Decorative note rewriting.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Three days</td><td>Write top fifteen questions once and revise formulas, diagrams and derivations.</td><td>Starting old topics.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>One day</td><td>Revise formula sheet, free body diagrams, SFD/BMD rules, centroid table method and stress-strain conversions.</td><td>Full textbook reading.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Exam morning</td><td>Only formulas, diagrams, definitions and priority questions.</td><td>Learning new derivations.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>During exam</td><td>Attempt known numericals first, draw diagrams large enough and keep units consistent.</td><td>Skipping diagrams in beam questions.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </section>

    <section class="pp-section">
      <h2>Summary And Golden Rules</h2>
      <p>The safest full-marks route in MOS is to master Modules 1 to 4. Module 1 builds statics and force systems, Module 2 gives the largest beam and stress-scoring area, Module 3 gives centroid, moment of inertia and torsion, and Module 4 gives repeated stress-strain numericals. Module 5 is mostly laboratory-hour content, so it is lower priority for theory except basic definitions and stress-strain diagram.</p>
      <ol class="pp-list">
        <li>Latest syllabus wins over old-paper habit.</li>
        <li>Every mechanics numerical must start with a correct diagram.</li>
        <li>Every beam answer needs reactions before shear-force and bending-moment diagrams.</li>
        <li>Every centroid and moment of inertia problem should use a table.</li>
        <li>Every stress-strain answer must show units clearly.</li>
        <li>For derivations, write assumptions before equations.</li>
        <li>Memorize bending equation, torsion equation and elastic-constant relations.</li>
        <li>Do not ignore definitions; they decide three-mark questions.</li>
        <li>Practice at least five numericals per high-weight topic.</li>
        <li>Make the examiner''s job easy: clean structure earns visible marks.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="pp-callout"><strong>Final HOD advice:</strong> A full-marks MOS answer looks like engineering work: correct free body diagram, correct sign convention, correct formula, clear unit and a boxed final answer.</div>
    </section>

