      <div class="pp-kicker">GTU BE - Engineering Graphics &amp; Design</div>
      <h1 class="pp-title">EGD <span>Full Marks Guide</span> for GTU Students</h1>
      <p class="pp-subtitle">A clean, exam-focused ShikshaSahayak guide for Engineering Graphics and Design. This blog converts the PDF into a professional revision page covering syllabus map, paper pattern, repeated questions, drawing strategy, formulas, definitions, construction logic, and last-minute plan.</p>
      <div class="pp-chip-row">
        <span class="pp-chip">M1 Introduction</span><span class="pp-chip">M2 Curves</span><span class="pp-chip">M3 Points &amp; Lines</span><span class="pp-chip">M4 Planes</span><span class="pp-chip">M5 Solids &amp; Sections</span><span class="pp-chip">M6 Orthographic</span><span class="pp-chip">M7 CAD</span>
      </div>
      <div class="pp-author-mini"><small>Prepared By</small><b>Ayush Pandey</b><div>Founder, ShikshaSahayak</div><div class="pp-small">B.S. Data Science and Applications | IIT Madras<br>B.E. Electrical Engineering | SSEC, Bhavnagar</div></div>
    </section>

    <div class="pp-grid">
      <div class="pp-card"><h3>Drawing-first subject</h3><p>EGD is not a memory subject. Marks come from method, scale accuracy, projection logic, neat final lines, and clear labels.</p></div>
      <div class="pp-card"><h3>Core scoring block</h3><p>Points and lines, planes, solids and sections, and orthographic views carry the highest practical scoring value.</p></div>
      <div class="pp-card"><h3>Do not ignore CAD</h3><p>Winter 2025 showed AutoCAD can appear as a full option. Prepare basic commands and modify commands.</p></div>
    </div>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>How This Report Was Prepared</h2><div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Use in this guide</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Latest syllabus</td><td>GTU Engineering Graphics and Design syllabus, subject code BE01R00061, course content and practical list.</td></tr><tr><td>New-code papers</td><td>Winter 2024, Summer 2025, and Winter 2025 EGD papers were used for strongest priority.</td></tr><tr><td>Question bank</td><td>Chapter-wise GTU-Verse question bank for BE01R00061 used to identify repeated seasons and question shapes.</td></tr><tr><td>Old papers</td><td>Subject code 3110013 used only for trend support where topics still appear in the latest syllabus.</td></tr><tr><td>Book reference</td><td>Engineering Drawing by B. Agrawal and C. M. Agrawal used for definitions, principles, and formula checks.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="pp-warning"><b>Important syllabus filter:</b> Development of Surfaces and Isometric Projections appear in older material/question-bank modules, but they are not part of the latest listed theory units. Treat them as low priority unless your faculty has specifically taught them.</div></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Subject Preparation Strategy</h2><p>Treat every chapter as a drawing algorithm: setup, reference line, assumed position, projection, rotation or auxiliary step, final outline, dimensions, and labels.</p><div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Focus</th><th>What to revise</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stage 1</td><td>Foundation</td><td>Lines, dimensioning, RF, scales, point positions, first-angle/third-angle basics.</td><td>1 day</td></tr><tr><td>Stage 2</td><td>Core scoring</td><td>Projection of lines, planes, solids, and section of solids. These decide the paper.</td><td>3-4 days</td></tr><tr><td>Stage 3</td><td>Visual speed</td><td>Orthographic sectional views and repeated paper figures. Practice view placement.</td><td>1-2 days</td></tr><tr><td>Stage 4</td><td>Theory finish</td><td>Definitions, differences, AutoCAD commands, conic terminology, curve applications.</td><td>0.5-1 day</td></tr><tr><td>Stage 5</td><td>Mock run</td><td>Solve one 70-mark paper with full line weight and labelling discipline.</td><td>2.5 hours</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Tips To Score Full Marks In EGD</h2><ul class="pp-list"><li>Do not leave 3-mark theory: lines, RF, section planes, solid classification, and AutoCAD advantages are fast marks.</li><li>For every drawing answer, write the given data first so the examiner sees that you understood the problem.</li><li>Use light construction lines and darker final lines. Construction lines should remain visible but not dominate.</li><li>Always label F.V., T.V., x-y line, H.P., V.P., true length, true shape, section plane, and final view direction.</li><li>In OR choices, choose the question with fewer orientation changes. A clean simple answer scores more than a messy ambitious answer.</li><li>Reserve the last 2 minutes of every long drawing for darkening, hidden lines, hatching, dimensions, and labels.</li><li>In orthographic, decide first angle or third angle before drawing. Wrong view placement can destroy marks.</li><li>For curves, tangent-normal marks depend on correct point selection and construction lines. Do not free-hand them.</li></ul></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Paper Presentation And Writing Tips</h2><ul class="pp-list"><li>Start each answer in a fresh clean area with enough spacing between drawings.</li><li>Draw the x-y/reference line first and keep it longer than needed.</li><li>Use exact units in captions. Unless the scale problem says otherwise, dimensions are in mm.</li><li>Hatching should be thin, uniform, and normally at 45 degrees.</li><li>For theory, use labelled points with a small sketch wherever possible.</li><li>For AutoCAD answers, write command name, use, and execution logic. Example: TRIM removes unwanted parts after selecting cutting edges.</li><li>For comparison questions, use a two-column table.</li></ul></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Latest Syllabus Map</h2><div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table"><thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Chapter</th><th>Latest syllabus focus</th><th>Exam signal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Introduction to Engineering Graphics</td><td>Drawing instruments, BIS-SP 46, line types, dimensioning, plane and diagonal scales, RF.</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Engineering Curves</td><td>Conics, cycloidal curves, involutes, spirals, applications and construction methods.</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Projections of Points and Lines</td><td>Quadrants, line inclination to HP/VP, true length, apparent length, inclinations.</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Projections of Planes</td><td>Polygon, circle, ellipse plates inclined to one or both reference planes.</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Projections and Sections of Solids</td><td>Prism, pyramid, cylinder, cone, frustum, section plane, true shape.</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Orthographic Projections</td><td>First/third angle, front/top/side views, sectional views from pictorial objects.</td><td>20%</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Computer Aided Drawing</td><td>AutoCAD basic 2D commands: line, circle, polyline, rectangle, hatch, fillet, chamfer, trim, extend, offset, dim style.</td><td>Lab + recent paper theory</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Observed New Paper Pattern</h3><div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table"><thead><tr><th>Paper</th><th>Topics appearing</th><th>Setter signal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Winter 2024</td><td>Scales, lines, ellipse, point projection, planes, solids, sections, orthographic sectional view.</td><td>Q5 gave 10 marks to orthographic view.</td></tr><tr><td>Summer 2025</td><td>Universal method, scales, spiral, points/lines, planes, solids, section, first/third angle, orthographic.</td><td>Very balanced paper. Lines, planes, and solids dominated.</td></tr><tr><td>Winter 2025</td><td>RF/diagonal scale, curves, points/lines, planes, solids/sections, orthographic, AutoCAD.</td><td>CAD became a full Q5 option. Do not ignore it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="pp-note"><b>Paper structure clue:</b> Most GTU EGD papers use 5 questions of 14 marks each. Subparts commonly split as 3 + 4 + 7 marks, while orthographic/CAD can appear as 4 + 10 or 3 + 4 + 7.</div></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Most Important Topics - Chapter Wise</h2><div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table"><thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Priority</th><th>Must-do topics</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Intro + Scales</td><td>Very High</td><td>RF, plain/diagonal scale, type of lines, dimensioning systems, universal method polygons.</td><td>Appears in all new-code papers.</td></tr><tr><td>Engineering Curves</td><td>Very High</td><td>Ellipse by rectangle/concentric/oblong, Archimedean/log spiral, conic terminology, involute/cycloid definitions.</td><td>At least one theory/drawing curve question in every new paper.</td></tr><tr><td>Points + Lines</td><td>Critical</td><td>Projection of points in quadrants; line inclined to HP and VP; true length and true inclinations.</td><td>Core 7-mark scoring area.</td></tr><tr><td>Planes</td><td>Critical</td><td>Triangle, pentagon, circular, rhombus, rectangular and semicircular plates with inclinations.</td><td>Usually 7 marks with inclination or true shape.</td></tr><tr><td>Solids</td><td>Very High</td><td>Cone/cylinder/pyramid/prism orientations; resting on generator/face; classify solids.</td><td>Regular 3/4/7 mark source.</td></tr><tr><td>Section of Solids</td><td>Very High</td><td>AIP section of prism/pyramid/cone; sectional top view; true shape of section.</td><td>Repeated as major 7-mark question.</td></tr><tr><td>Orthographic</td><td>Critical</td><td>First angle view placement, sectional elevation, plan, side view, projection symbols.</td><td>Often final high-value question.</td></tr><tr><td>AutoCAD</td><td>Rising</td><td>Advantages, applications, modify panel, command symbols, trim/extend/fillet/chamfer/offset.</td><td>Strong in Winter 2025; easy theory marks.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Most Repeated Questions - Chapter Wise</h2><h3>Introduction / Scales / Dimensioning</h3><ol class="pp-num-list"><li>Construct diagonal scale from RF and mark a given distance.</li><li>Construct map/plain scale when actual distance and drawing length are given.</li><li>Define RF and classify scales.</li><li>Explain type of lines with applications and symbols.</li><li>Differentiate aligned and unidirectional dimensioning.</li><li>Draw regular pentagon/hexagon by universal method or divide a line into equal parts.</li></ol><h3>Engineering Curves</h3><ol class="pp-num-list"><li>Draw ellipse by rectangle method, concentric circle method, or oblong method.</li><li>Define ellipse, cycloid, involute, conic section and eccentricity.</li><li>Classify engineering curves and give applications.</li><li>Draw Archimedean spiral with tangent and normal.</li><li>Construct logarithmic spiral for one convolution.</li><li>Give conic terminology with sketch.</li></ol><h3>Points and Lines</h3><ol class="pp-num-list"><li>Draw projections of points in different quadrants on the same x-y line.</li><li>Line of given true length inclined to HP and VP.</li><li>Line problem with given end projectors and endpoint distances; find true length.</li><li>Line with point P dividing AB in ratio AP:PB.</li><li>Spatial distance problem like the two-lemons question.</li><li>Define quadrant, AIP, and draw simple line positions.</li></ol><h3>Planes</h3><ol class="pp-num-list"><li>Triangular/pentagonal plate resting on HP with surface inclined to HP.</li><li>Circular plate whose plan becomes ellipse; find inclination.</li><li>Rhombus or rectangular plate whose plan becomes a square.</li><li>Semicircular plate resting on HP on diameter.</li><li>Set-square plane projection.</li><li>Square plate resting on corner with diagonals at given inclinations.</li></ol><h3>Solids and Sections</h3><ol class="pp-num-list"><li>Classify solids with sketches.</li><li>Differentiate prism vs pyramid, cube vs square prism, square pyramid vs tetrahedron.</li><li>Cone resting on one generator on HP.</li><li>Cylinder/cone/pyramid with axis inclined to one plane and parallel to the other.</li><li>Pentagonal/hexagonal pyramid or prism cut by AIP; draw sectional view and true shape.</li><li>Cone cut by AIP inclined to HP and passing through axis at a given distance.</li></ol><h3>Orthographic and CAD</h3><ol class="pp-num-list"><li>Draw sectional elevation, top view, and side view from pictorial object.</li><li>Compare first angle and third angle projection.</li><li>Draw symbols for projection methods.</li><li>Explain orthographic projection and classify views.</li><li>List AutoCAD modify commands and write use/execution.</li><li>Write AutoCAD advantages/applications over manual drawing.</li></ol></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Combined 15 Most Important Questions</h2><div class="pp-success"><b>How to use this:</b> Finish Priority 1 first. These questions cover the highest-return drawing logic. Priority 2 builds marks across chapters. Priority 3 protects theory and recent CAD marks.</div><div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table"><thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Question type</th><th>Exam value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P1</td><td>Line inclined to HP and VP; draw projections and find true length/inclinations.</td><td>Critical</td></tr><tr><td>P1</td><td>Projection of points in all quadrants plus simple line positions.</td><td>Critical</td></tr><tr><td>P1</td><td>Projection of planes: pentagonal/circular/rhombus plate inclined to HP/VP.</td><td>Critical</td></tr><tr><td>P1</td><td>Orthographic sectional view from pictorial object using first angle method.</td><td>Critical</td></tr><tr><td>P1</td><td>Section of prism/pyramid/cone by AIP and true shape of section.</td><td>Critical</td></tr><tr><td>P2</td><td>Diagonal/plain scale from RF or map data; mark the asked distance.</td><td>Very high</td></tr><tr><td>P2</td><td>Ellipse by rectangle/concentric/oblong method with tangent-normal awareness.</td><td>Very high</td></tr><tr><td>P2</td><td>Engineering curves: classification, conic terminology, spiral/cycloid/involute.</td><td>Very high</td></tr><tr><td>P2</td><td>Projection of solids: cone/cylinder/pyramid resting on generator/face.</td><td>Very high</td></tr><tr><td>P2</td><td>First angle vs third angle projection with symbols and view placement.</td><td>Very high</td></tr><tr><td>P3</td><td>Type of lines, symbols, BIS conventions, aligned vs unidirectional dimensioning.</td><td>Good support</td></tr><tr><td>P3</td><td>Solid classification and differences: prism/pyramid, cube/square prism, tetrahedron.</td><td>Good support</td></tr><tr><td>P3</td><td>Universal method for pentagon/hexagon and divide a line into equal parts.</td><td>Good support</td></tr><tr><td>P3</td><td>AutoCAD advantages, applications, modify commands and command symbols.</td><td>Rising</td></tr><tr><td>P3</td><td>Section plane types, apparent section, true shape of section definitions.</td><td>Good support</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Last-Moment Skip List</h2><div class="pp-warning"><b>Disclaimer - skip at your own risk:</b> This list is only for badly short time. If your faculty has stressed any topic below, study it even if it appears low priority here.</div><ul class="pp-list"><li>Development of Surfaces: appears in old syllabus/question bank, but not in latest listed EGD theory units.</li><li>Isometric Projections: strong in old material, but not in latest listed EGD theory units.</li><li>Intersection of Surfaces, Oblique Projection, Perspective Projection: book chapters, not part of latest listed GTU EGD unit map.</li><li>Highly specific old loci mechanisms: slider-crank and unusual link mechanism loci are lower than ellipse/spiral/cycloid basics.</li><li>Advanced AutoCAD interface details: focus first on basic and modify commands.</li><li>Vernier/comparative/scale-of-chords details: plain and diagonal scales with RF are more urgent.</li></ul></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Most Important Formulas And Principles</h2><div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Formula / Principle</th><th>Exam use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Representative Fraction</td><td>\(RF= rac{	ext{drawing length}}{	ext{actual length}}\)</td><td>Both lengths must be in same units. RF has no unit.</td></tr><tr><td>Scale length</td><td>\(	ext{Scale length}=RF	imes	ext{maximum actual length}\)</td><td>Use cm/mm after unit conversion.</td></tr><tr><td>Area/volume scale</td><td>\(RF=\sqrt{ rac{	ext{drawing area}}{	ext{actual area}}}\), \(RF=\sqrt[3]{ rac{	ext{drawing volume}}{	ext{actual volume}}}\)</td><td>Useful for rare area/volume scale questions.</td></tr><tr><td>Ellipse focal relation</td><td>\(a^2=b^2+c^2\), \(e= rac{c}{a}\)</td><td>Major axis = \(2a\), minor axis = \(2b\), distance between foci = \(2c\).</td></tr><tr><td>Conic eccentricity</td><td>\(e&lt;1\) ellipse, \(e=1\) parabola, \(e&gt;1\) hyperbola</td><td>Eccentricity is the key classifier.</td></tr><tr><td>Cycloid base</td><td>\(	ext{one revolution length}=\pi D\)</td><td>D is rolling circle diameter.</td></tr><tr><td>True length principle</td><td>A line parallel to a plane shows true length on that plane.</td><td>If inclined to both HP and VP, use rotation/auxiliary construction.</td></tr><tr><td>True shape principle</td><td>A plane parallel to a reference plane shows true shape on that plane.</td><td>Inclined plane appears foreshortened.</td></tr><tr><td>Section true shape</td><td>Project apparent section on a plane parallel to section plane.</td><td>Especially for AIP/AVP sections.</td></tr><tr><td>First angle rule</td><td>Object is between observer and plane.</td><td>Top view below front view; right side view placed on left.</td></tr><tr><td>Third angle rule</td><td>Plane is between observer and object.</td><td>Top view above front view; right side view placed on right.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Most Important Definitions</h2><div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition to write in exam</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RF</td><td>Ratio of drawing length to actual length, both expressed in the same units.</td></tr><tr><td>Plain scale</td><td>Scale used to read two consecutive units, such as metre and decimetre.</td></tr><tr><td>Diagonal scale</td><td>Scale used to read three consecutive units with greater accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>Conic section</td><td>Curve obtained by cutting a right circular cone by a plane in different positions.</td></tr><tr><td>Ellipse</td><td>Conic with eccentricity less than 1; sum of distances from two foci is constant.</td></tr><tr><td>Parabola</td><td>Conic with eccentricity equal to 1; point moves equidistant from focus and directrix.</td></tr><tr><td>Hyperbola</td><td>Conic with eccentricity greater than 1.</td></tr><tr><td>Cycloid</td><td>Curve traced by a point on a circle rolling on a straight line without slipping.</td></tr><tr><td>Involute</td><td>Curve traced by the free end of a taut string unwound from a circle or polygon.</td></tr><tr><td>Archimedean spiral</td><td>Spiral in which radius vector changes uniformly with angle.</td></tr><tr><td>True length</td><td>Actual length of a line, seen when the line is parallel to the projection plane.</td></tr><tr><td>AIP</td><td>Auxiliary inclined plane; perpendicular to VP and inclined to HP.</td></tr><tr><td>AVP</td><td>Auxiliary vertical plane; perpendicular to HP and inclined to VP.</td></tr><tr><td>Section plane</td><td>Imaginary cutting plane used to reveal internal details of a solid.</td></tr><tr><td>True shape of section</td><td>Actual shape and size of the cut surface, projected on a plane parallel to the cutting plane.</td></tr><tr><td>Orthographic projection</td><td>Parallel projection where projectors are perpendicular to the projection plane.</td></tr><tr><td>Prism</td><td>Solid with two equal parallel polygonal bases joined by rectangular faces.</td></tr><tr><td>Pyramid</td><td>Solid with polygonal base and triangular faces meeting at an apex.</td></tr><tr><td>Frustum</td><td>Remaining part of a cone/pyramid after cutting by a plane parallel to base.</td></tr><tr><td>Truncated solid</td><td>Solid cut by a plane not necessarily parallel to the base.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Most Important Construction Logic</h2><div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Construction</th><th>Must write / follow</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>RF derivation</td><td>Bring drawing length and actual length to same units, reduce the ratio, then use \(	ext{scale length}=RF	imes	ext{maximum distance}\).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Ellipse from foci/minor axis</td><td>Take \(c\) as half distance between foci, \(b\) as half minor axis, then \(a=\sqrt{b^2+c^2}\), so major axis is \(2a\).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Cycloid construction</td><td>Rolling circle covers one circumference in one revolution, so directing line is \(\pi D\).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Archimedean spiral</td><td>Divide angular travel and radial change into equal parts; join smooth locus points.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Line inclined to both planes</td><td>Draw true length in simple position, rotate/transfer views to satisfy second inclination, then locate final projections.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Projection of planes</td><td>Start with true shape where easiest, tilt to first inclination, then rotate to satisfy second condition.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Section true shape</td><td>Mark section points, project to auxiliary reference parallel to section plane, then join in order.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Orthographic conversion</td><td>Identify front direction, transfer widths/depths consistently, draw visible outline first, then hidden/section lines.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Last-Minute Preparation Strategy</h2><div class="pp-table-wrap"><table class="pp-table"><thead><tr><th>Time left</th><th>Action</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>24 hours left</td><td>Finish scales, points/lines, planes, solids/sections, orthographic; read theory at night.</td><td>Do one full paper layout.</td></tr><tr><td>12 hours left</td><td>Practice one line problem, one plane, one solid, one section, one orthographic. Memorize theory tables.</td><td>Avoid brand-new obscure questions.</td></tr><tr><td>6 hours left</td><td>Priority 1 list only + RF formulas + first/third angle + AutoCAD commands.</td><td>Revise with pencil in hand.</td></tr><tr><td>1 hour left</td><td>Read definitions, comparison tables, section plane types, line/scale formulas.</td><td>Do not start a long drawing now.</td></tr><tr><td>Inside exam</td><td>Attempt sure 3/4-mark parts first, then 7/10-mark drawings. Keep OR selection practical.</td><td>Final 10 minutes for labels/darkening.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Top 30 Most Expected Questions</h2><ol class="pp-num-list"><li>Construct a diagonal scale from RF and mark the asked distance.</li><li>Construct a map/plain scale from given actual distance and drawing length.</li><li>Define Representative Fraction and classify scales.</li><li>Draw regular pentagon and hexagon by universal method; divide a line into equal parts.</li><li>List types of engineering lines with applications and symbols.</li><li>Differentiate aligned and unidirectional dimensioning systems.</li><li>Give conic terminology with sketch and classify conics by eccentricity.</li><li>Draw ellipse by rectangle method.</li><li>Draw ellipse by concentric circle or oblong method when axes/foci are given.</li><li>Draw Archimedean spiral with tangent and normal.</li><li>Construct logarithmic spiral for one convolution.</li><li>Define involute, ellipse, cycloid and give applications.</li><li>Draw projections of points in all quadrants on a common x-y line.</li><li>Draw a line of given true length inclined to HP and VP.</li><li>Find true length and true inclinations from given end projectors/end distances.</li><li>Solve a line division problem where point P divides AB in a given ratio.</li><li>Solve real-distance spatial problem similar to the two-lemons question.</li><li>Projection of triangular/set-square plane inclined to HP/VP.</li><li>Projection of pentagonal plate resting on corner/edge with surface inclined.</li><li>Projection of circular plane whose plan appears as ellipse; find inclination.</li><li>Projection of rhombus/rectangle whose plan becomes a square.</li><li>Projection of semicircular plate resting on HP on diameter.</li><li>Classify solids and differentiate prism vs pyramid / cube vs square prism.</li><li>Projection of cone resting on one generator on HP.</li><li>Projection of square/hexagonal pyramid lying on face or slant edge.</li><li>Projection of cylinder/cone with axis inclined to one plane and parallel to other.</li><li>Section of pentagonal/hexagonal pyramid or prism by AIP with true shape.</li><li>Section of cone by AIP; draw front view, sectional top view, and true shape.</li><li>Draw orthographic views/sectional elevation from pictorial object using first angle.</li><li>Write AutoCAD advantages/applications and explain modify commands with use/execution.</li></ol></section>

    <section class="pp-section"><h2>Summary And Golden Rules</h2><ul class="pp-list"><li>EGD rewards clean process more than decoration. Accuracy, labels, and view relation are everything.</li><li>Never skip points/lines, planes, solids/sections, orthographic, scales, and ellipse/spiral basics.</li><li>Theory questions are not optional. They can recover marks when one drawing goes wrong.</li><li>Latest new-code papers show CAD can appear. Learn at least advantages, applications, and modify commands.</li><li>Old-syllabus topics should not distract you from the latest syllabus, but do not ignore your classroom notes.</li><li>A neat 80% correct drawing often scores more than a crowded 100% attempted drawing with unclear final answer.</li></ul><div class="pp-note"><b>Final HOD-style advice:</b> In EGD, the examiner cannot give marks for what cannot be read. Keep construction light, final answer dark, labels complete, and assumptions written.</div></section>

